-Before editing a framework or scientific-library change, perform evidence-gated repository localization: first write a short hypothesis about the failure, then verify it by tracing the reported input through the concrete call chain to the failing output. Record the exact files, symbols, and conditions that confirm or falsify the hypothesis; do not commit to a fix based only on similarly named code. Identify the primary implementation, every relevant caller, subclasses, alternate backends, serializers/parsers, configuration defaults, and existing regression tests. Use search and file inspection to verify each dependency rather than inferring it from names. Do not edit until you can cite the concrete code path from the reported input to the observed behavior and distinguish the likely root cause from nearby symptoms. Trace the relevant data and control flow, state the likely root cause, and identify the smallest compatible change. Then build a behavior matrix before editing: normal inputs; empty, null, missing, malformed, and partially specified inputs; minimum, maximum, zero, negative, overflow, underflow, and exact-boundary values; subclasses and alternate implementations; omitted versus explicit defaults; expected exceptions and messages; and interactions with existing public APIs. For every row, compare the old and new behavior explicitly, including return values, exception types and messages, ordering, mutation, numerical precision, serialization, and subclass or alternate-backend behavior. Mark each difference as required or accidental, add a focused regression test for every required difference, and add compatibility tests for nearby unchanged rows while preserving unrelated behavior. After every nontrivial edit, run the narrowest relevant test or a focused reproduction, inspect the complete failure output, and iterate on the implementation until the focused checks pass. Then run the broader relevant test groups and, before finalizing, perform an adversarial compatibility review covering public API signatures, exception types and messages, ordering, mutability, numerical precision, serialization, subclass behavior, and interactions with neighboring features; reject any change lacking evidence for intentional behavior differences.
